ROBench 1.01 (18-Oct-2026)
==========================

A benchmark for RISC OS 5: processor, floating point, memory, SWIs,
files, graphics and BBC BASIC, measured the way the standard benchmarks
measure, so that two machines (a Raspberry Pi 4 and RISC OS on Linux,
say) can be compared test by test.

Running it
----------
Double-click !ROBench. It runs single-tasking, as a game does, so that
other tasks can't disturb the timing; the graphics tests draw all over
the screen. A full run takes about 15 minutes; the results open in your
text editor when it finishes, and are kept in !ROBench.Results.

  Quick      the same tests, shorter (about 4 minutes, less precise)
  CPUOnly    processor and memory tests only (also fine in a task window)

To name the machine in the results, set ROBench$Machine first, e.g.
  *Set ROBench$Machine Pi 4 1.8GHz
To compare with an earlier run, from the command line:
  *<ROBench$Dir>.robench -g -ref <ROBench$Dir>.Refs.MacVM
(Refs holds RISC OS on Linux results: MacVM, as the UTM image runs on a
Mac Studio, and Testbedx86.)
which adds a ratio column and a geometric mean per group. *robench -h
lists the options.

How it measures
---------------
- Each test is calibrated: the iteration count doubles until a run takes
  an eighth of the target time (this also warms the caches), then is
  scaled so that a timed run lasts 2 seconds (the clock ticks in
  centiseconds, so +-0.5% at worst).
- Each test is then run 5 times. The median is reported, with the best
  and the spread ((slowest - fastest) / median: high means something
  else was taking time).
- Every test returns a checksum that depends on all its results, so the
  compiler can't leave work out, and every run must give the same one.
  Where there is a known answer it is checked: SHA-256 and CRC-32 test
  vectors, LINPACK's residual, the FFT round trip, the compressor's
  round trip, N-queens' 724 solutions, Whetstone's result, the N-body
  energy. A wrong answer shows as FAILED.
- The C tests are built with GCC 10 at -O2 for ARMv7 with VFPv3
  (riscos-crossdev), statically linked with UnixLib; the neon tests
  with NEON intrinsics.

Changes
-------
1.01: the neon tests; -noneon.

The tests
---------
int.coremark    list, matrix, state machine and CRC work in the style of
                CoreMark (not CoreMark: its numbers aren't CoreMark scores)
int.compress    LZ77 (LZ4-style) compression of 1 MB of English-like text
int.decompress  and decompression, checked against the original
int.sha256      SHA-256 of 1 MB
int.crc32       CRC-32 of 1 MB, a byte at a time from a table
int.sort        quicksort of 256K random 32-bit numbers
int.wordfreq    counting the words of 1 MB of text in a hash table
int.queens      N-queens on a 10 x 10 board (recursion, branches)
fp.whetstone    Whetstone, double precision (MWIPS)
fp.linpack      LINPACK 200 x 200, double precision (MFLOPS)
fp.fft          16K-point complex FFT, forward and back (MFLOPS)
fp.nbody        the five-planet N-body simulation
fp.mandelbrot   Mandelbrot set 320 x 240, single precision
fp.matmul       128 x 128 single precision matrix multiply (MFLOPS)
neon.blend      alpha blending 640 x 480 RGBA, 8-bit (NEON intrinsics)
neon.yuv2rgb    YUV 4:2:0 to RGBA 640 x 480, 16-bit fixed point
neon.sad        motion search: 16 x 16 sums of absolute differences
neon.saxpy      y = a*x + y on 64K floats
neon.fir        32-tap FIR filter on 16K float samples
neon.xform      4 x 4 matrix transform of 64K float vertices
                (every NEON test is checked against plain C; without
                NEON, or with -noneon, the plain C runs instead)
mem.copy..triad STREAM-style copy, scale, add, triad on 8 MB arrays
mem.memcpy      the C library's memcpy and memset, 8 MB
mem.read*       reading a 16 KB, 256 KB and 8 MB working set
mem.lat*        latency: following a random chain of pointers through
                16 KB, 256 KB, 4 MB and 32 MB (nanoseconds, lower is better)
mem.malloc      malloc and free, mixed sizes
os.swi          a SWI (XOS_ReadMonotonicTime) from assembler
os.readvar      OS_ReadVarVal through the C library's _swix
io.*            8 MB files with fwrite/fread and OS_File, byte streams
                with OS_BPut/OS_BGet, creating and deleting small files
                (in <Wimp$ScrapDir>; includes the file system's caching)
gfx.*           filled rectangles (with ColourTrans), lines, block copy,
                sprites plain and scaled, anti-aliased Font_Paint, Draw
                fill and stroke, JPEG decode and plot (SpriteExtend), all
                in the current screen mode: results depend on it, so
                compare machines in the same mode
basic.*         BBC BASIC V: loops, real maths, trig, strings, PROC/FN,
                sorting, sieve; basic64.* the same in BASIC64 (BASICVFP)

Comparing
---------
Like with like: the same version of ROBench, screen mode, and file
system. The ratio column and geometric means come from -ref.

